 ## 10. What still looks placeholder
 
-{{PLACEHOLDER}}
+Plainly: none of this is production quality. The scenes are a big step from G1's placeholder art (the sheets of §5),
+and what still looks placeholder is:
+
+- **Motion.** Every character in A–D moves by the stage-chars CMU retargets (PROTOTYPE-ONLY) plus the stage-kit
+  authored clips (guard, thrust, cuts, block, hit, deaths, surrender, victory). Crowd soldiers play one clip or a
+  blend of two; there is no foot IK on slopes and no collision, so at the contact line spears pass through shields
+  and bodies. Formations in B only idle.
+- **Close-ups (A, D).** Faces are the stage-chars MakeHuman identities: skin from a 512-texel albedo with procedural
+  detail, a painted hair cap with shells and cards, simple mouth interiors. At the closest framings the skin is smooth
+  and the hair reads as a cap. Expressions are blends of a few face pose units.
+- **Crowds (B, C, D).** Beyond a few metres soldiers are the outfits' reduced meshes (render2's levels); at vista
+  distance a formation is a field of small silhouettes. All 2,100 soldiers of C are heavy infantry with spears or
+  swords: the battle has no missile troops or cavalry, and kits are four per force by formation. The era mixes: scene
+  A is a Classical hoplite line, B–D a medieval kit.
+- **The aftermath (D).** Smoke and dust are soft billboards, not volumes; at a distance a plume reads as a few soft
+  balls. Dead soldiers lie in ground cover that partly hides them; there is no trampled ground, no blood, no wreckage
+  beyond dropped shields. The victors' soldiers whom the trace records as Surrendered kneel like the defeated (the
+  literal reading of the trace). The hero pair's two-shot is busy with soldiers standing between.
+- **Settlements (E).** The stronghold is the env structure kit's walls, towers and houses (tiling stone, plaster and
+  tile materials), on a bare cleared glacis; the village huts are simple cones; nobody lives in either (no people,
+  animals, hearth smoke). Beyond the 1 km map the land is the render1 far field (a flat plane and a band of hills).
+- **Weather.** Rain is thin camera-facing streaks (no splashes, drips or wet-darkened cloth that changes over time);
+  puddles appear only on level terrain; the overcast sky is a uniform blend, not clouds; mist is a height fog with no
+  light shafts; trees sway the same in every weather.
+- **Shadows** at low sun show cascade acne bands on smooth ground in single interactive frames (render1's cascades;
+  accumulated stills average them out).
 
 ## 11. Limitations and risks
 
-{{LIMITS}}
+- **No hardware evidence.** Every number is llvmpipe on a shared, heavily loaded machine; only the Victus run measures
+  these scenes on the reference GPU. Loads of 70–90 s here are mostly the tree impostor bake on the CPU rasterizer.
+- **The battle adapter is a prototype choice.** The grid downscale, the village as Building cells, the site search and
+  the deployment are this tool's, not a ruled design; a different seed or site gives a different battle. The resolver
+  ran unmodified, so the battle shows what the prototype resolver does today (for example 151 soldiers of the winning
+  side end the battle Surrendered).
+- **`G2BT` is a prototype format** for this presentation's traces, not a save or replay format.
+- **Scene D's binding.** Slot D measures the Shown state (the upper bound); the product default of the death-content
+  setting is open (D-079 Q12) and nothing here decides it.
+- **CMU motion** is PROTOTYPE-ONLY: every capture of A–D says so; shipping needs authored or licensed motion.
+- **Weather is a look**, never read by any simulation, and it is not tied to any campaign weather state.
+- **Era mix and content gaps** (no ranged or mounted troops in C) limit what the benchmark exercises: archery volleys,
+  horses and siege engines are not measured.
 
 ## 12. Sim/presentation boundary
 
-{{BOUNDARY}}
+- **No simulation code changed.** `git diff 21a181e -- prototypes/m7-sprint1` is empty; the exporter references
+  `Proto.M7` read-only and drives the resolver only through its public surface. Nothing outside `prototypes/m7-gfx2`
+  changed, and G1 is untouched (`git diff 1601c74 -- prototypes/m7-gfx1` is empty).
+- **Presentation reads, never writes.** The scenes read the authoritative grid and the battle trace; `BattleTrace` has
+  no mutators and a test updates the trace-driven crowd at three snapshot times and checks the trace hashes the same
+  afterwards (§8). Weather, time of day, smoke, the aftermath plan and every camera are presentation only (DR42); the
+  hero characters of scene D take two trace soldiers' positions and states and nothing else (DR39, DR42).
+- **Rendering does not feed back.** No value computed by the renderer reaches the trace, the grid or the exporter; the
+  exporter's only inputs are the fixture grid, the village plan (a presentation plan, entering as Building cells: the
+  PROTOTYPE ADAPTER) and its own constants.
+- **Determinism.** `scripts/check-gfx-determinism.sh` (integer-only scan of `Gfx.Proc/Authoritative`): OK, 4 files.
+  The exporter is integer-only; its output is reproducible ({{EXPORTCHECK}}). Smoke puffs and rain streaks are pure
+  functions of their index and the scene time; the aftermath and vantage solvers use integer costs with a fixed
+  candidate order.
 
